begin model

#@title: COVID-19 regional epidemic forecasting (Lin et al., 2021)

#@description: |
#  Deterministic compartmental (SEIR-type) model of a regional COVID-19 epidemic,
#  parameterized here for the New York City metropolitan statistical area
#  (New York-Newark-Jersey City, NY-NJ-PA). Susceptibles progress
#  S -> E1..E5 (a five-stage, non-exponential/Erlang incubation period) ->
#  A (asymptomatic) or I (mildly symptomatic) -> H (hospitalized/severe) ->
#  R (recovered) / D (deceased). Each population also carries a behavioral state:
#  M (mixing) <-> P (protected by social distancing), plus Q (quarantined via
#  testing/contact-tracing at rate kQ or symptom-driven self-isolation at rate
#  jQ). Social distancing switches on at day sigma and relaxes toward a setpoint
#  p with timescale 1/lambda; this variant uses TWO phases (n=1): (p0, lambda0)
#  from sigma and (p1, lambda1) from tau1. A counter() molecule integrates time
#  into the observable t, which the if(t>=...) rate-law functions read to gate the
#  time-dependent behavior. Transmission is a mass-action force of infection
#  beta*(infectious)/S0 acting on S; the fit observable fDCs_Cum is the cumulative
#  count of detected symptomatic cases, whose day-to-day increment is the daily
#  new-case forecast. Behavior: an epidemic wave whose growth is bent down by
#  social distancing into the broad spring-2020 NYC plateau. Parameters hold the
#  authors' published maximum-a-posteriori estimate.

#@keyword: |
#  epidemiology, COVID-19, SARS-CoV-2, compartmental model, SEIR, social
#  distancing, quarantine, non-exponential incubation, Erlang, mass action,
#  forecasting, Bayesian inference, Homo sapiens

#@reference: |
#  Lin YT, Neumann J, Miller EF, Posner RG, Mallela A, Safta C, Ray J, Thakur G,
#  Chinthavali S, Hlavacek WS (2021). Daily Forecasting of Regional Epidemics of
#  Coronavirus Disease with Bayesian Uncertainty Quantification, United States.
#  Emerg Infect Dis 27(3):767-778. doi:10.3201/eid2703.203364 -- the model, the
#  fixed epidemiological constants (Tables 2-3), and the NYC ("BigApple") fit
#  reproduced here (Fig 3).
#  Mallela A, Lin YT, Hlavacek WS (2023). Differential contagiousness of
#  respiratory disease across the United States. Epidemics 45:100718.
#  doi:10.1016/j.epidem.2023.100718 -- the single-phase (n=0) per-MSA form used
#  in the sibling variant file.

#@note: |
#  PROVENANCE. Molecule types, seed species, observables, functions, and rules are
#  the authors' own PyBioNetFit model (PyBNF examples/COVID19forecasting_aMCMC/
#  BigApple/m1.bngl), house-styled here. The published NYC forecast (Lin et al.
#  2021 Fig 3) is single-phase (n=0); this two-phase (n=1) "BigApple" fit is the
#  authors' extended example whose first phase reproduces the published MAP and
#  whose second phase (onset ~day 143) captures the later plateau over the full
#  t=0..170 window. Fitted-parameter nominals are the published MAP
#  (BigApple/adaptive_files/MLE_params.txt); the single-phase (n=0) form is the
#  sibling variant covid19_regional_epidemic_forecasting_lin2021_singlephase.bngl.
#  Adapted from the validated pybnf-jobs/Lin-2021/nyc_multiphase job.
#
#  UNITS. Populations are counts of individuals; the model is inherently
#  population-based (no molar concentration), so no NA / reference-volume
#  conversion applies. The negative-binomial dispersion r used to score the fit
#  lives only in the likelihood, not the model, and is omitted here.

begin parameters

  # Reporting period for daily incidence
  delta_t 1 # d
  # Number of ADDITIONAL social-distancing periods (n+1 total); n=1 = two phases
  n 1 # dimensionless

  # === Fitted parameters: authors' published MAP (BigApple/MLE_params.txt) ===
  # Start time of the regional epidemic (model day 0 = 2020-01-21)
  t0 32.831672 # d
  # Offset from t0 to the 1st social-distancing onset
  t_delta 0.072681 # d
  # Onset of the 1st social-distancing period (~2020-02-23)
  sigma t0+t_delta # d
  # Steady-state social-distancing setpoint, phase 1
  p0 0.872484 # dimensionless
  # Social-distancing relaxation eigenvalue, phase 1
  lambda0 0.098304 # /d
  # Offset from sigma to the 2nd social-distancing onset
  t_delta2 110.222846 # d
  # Onset of the 2nd social-distancing period (~2020-06-12)
  tau1 t0+t_delta+t_delta2 # d
  # Steady-state social-distancing setpoint, phase 2
  p1 0.736221 # dimensionless
  # Social-distancing relaxation eigenvalue, phase 2
  lambda1 5.180963 # /d
  # Transmission rate constant (mixing population)
  beta 2.033369 # /d
  # Fraction of mild symptomatic cases detected
  fD 0.114511 # dimensionless

  # === Fixed parameters (Lin et al. 2021 Tables 2-3) ===
  # Total NYC-MSA (NY-NJ-PA) population (2019 census estimate)
  S0 19216182 # individuals
  # Initial mildly symptomatic infected
  I0 1 # individuals
  # Protective effect of social distancing
  mb 0.1 # dimensionless
  # Relative infectiousness of E (presymptomatic) vs I
  rhoE 1.1 # dimensionless
  # Relative infectiousness of A (asymptomatic) vs I
  rhoA 0.9 # dimensionless
  # Incubation-stage progression rate (5-stage Erlang)
  kL 0.94 # /d
  # Quarantine rate (testing / contact tracing)
  kQ 0.0038 # /d
  # Self-isolation rate (symptom awareness)
  jQ 0.4 # /d
  # Fraction of exposed who never develop symptoms
  fA 0.44 # dimensionless
  # Fraction of symptomatic progressing to severe / hospitalization
  fH 0.054 # dimensionless
  # Fraction of hospitalized who recover
  fR 0.79 # dimensionless
  # A -> R clearance rate
  cA 0.26 # /d
  # I -> H or I -> R rate
  cI 0.12 # /d
  # H -> R (or H -> D) rate
  cH 0.17 # /d

end parameters

begin molecule types

  # Simulation clock: 0->counter() at rate 1 integrates time; observable t = counter
  counter()
  # Susceptible; M = mixing, P = protected (social-distanced)
  S(state~M~P)
  # Incubation stages 1-5 (non-exponential Erlang latency); Q = quarantined
  E1(state~M~P~Q)
  E2(state~M~P~Q)
  E3(state~M~P~Q)
  E4(state~M~P~Q)
  E5(state~M~P~Q)
  # Asymptomatic infectious
  A(state~M~P~Q)
  # Mildly symptomatic infectious
  I(state~M~P~Q)
  # Hospitalized / severe
  H()
  # Recovered
  R()
  # Deceased
  D()
  # Cumulative detected-case counter (integral of the detection rate)
  fDCs()

end molecule types

begin seed species

  counter() 1 # dimensionless
  S(state~M) S0 # individuals
  I(state~M) I0 # individuals
  fDCs() 0 # individuals

end seed species

begin observables

  # Susceptibles by behavioral state
  Molecules SM S(state~M)
  Molecules SP S(state~P)
  Molecules nStotal S()
  # Incubation-stage populations (M/P/Q) used by the force of infection and detection
  Molecules E1M E1(state~M)
  Molecules E1P E1(state~P)
  Molecules E2M E2(state~M)
  Molecules E2P E2(state~P)
  Molecules E2to5M E2(state~M) E3(state~M) E4(state~M) E5(state~M)
  Molecules E2to5P E2(state~P) E3(state~P) E4(state~P) E5(state~P)
  Molecules E2to5Q E2(state~Q) E3(state~Q) E4(state~Q) E5(state~Q)
  Molecules nE2_MP E2(state~M) E2(state~P)
  Molecules nE3_MP E3(state~M) E3(state~P)
  Molecules nE4_MP E4(state~M) E4(state~P)
  Molecules nE5_MP E5(state~M) E5(state~P)
  # Infectious populations
  Molecules IM I(state~M)
  Molecules IP I(state~P)
  Molecules IQ I(state~Q)
  Molecules AM A(state~M)
  Molecules AP A(state~P)
  Molecules AQ A(state~Q)
  # Outcomes and the simulation clock
  Molecules H H()
  Molecules R R()
  Molecules D D()
  Molecules t counter()
  # Fit observable: cumulative detected symptomatic cases
  Molecules fDCs_Cum fDCs()

end observables

begin functions

  # Epidemic switched on at t0
  U()=if(t>=t0,1,0)
  # Two-phase social-distancing eigenvalue and setpoint (0 before sigma)
  Lambda()=if(t>=sigma && t<tau1,lambda0,if(t>=tau1 && t<200,lambda1,0))
  P()=if(t>=sigma && t<tau1,p0,if(t>=tau1 && t<200,p1,0))
  # Quarantine / self-isolation / clearance switched on at t0 (mixing branch)
  kQfunc()=if(t>=t0,kQ,0)
  jQfunc()=if(t>=t0,jQ,0)
  cIfunc()=if(t>=t0,cI,0)
  # Force of infection contributions (infectious weighted by relative infectiousness)
  phiM()=if(t>=t0,IM+rhoE*E2to5M+rhoA*AM,0)
  phiP()=IP+rhoE*E2to5P+rhoA*AP
  # Cumulative case measures (informational output)
  CumNum_symptomatic_cases()=IM+IP+IQ+H+(1-fA)*R+D
  CumNum_detected_cases_Cum()=fD*(IM+IP+IQ+H+(1-fA)*R+D)
  CumNum_all_cases()=fD*(IM+IP+IQ+AM+AP+AQ+H+R+D)

end functions

begin reaction rules

  # Cumulative detected symptomatic cases (integral of the detection rate)
  0->fDCs() fD*(1-fA)*kL*nE5_MP
  # Simulation clock (integrates time into the observable t)
  0->counter() 1

  # Disease transmission (mass-action force of infection on susceptibles)
  S(state~M)->E1(state~M) (beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())
  S(state~P)->E1(state~P) mb*(beta/S0)*(phiM()+mb*phiP())

  # Social distancing: mixing <-> protected for every non-quarantined population
  S(state~M)<->S(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  E1(state~M)<->E1(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  E2(state~M)<->E2(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  E3(state~M)<->E3(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  E4(state~M)<->E4(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  E5(state~M)<->E5(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  A(state~M)<->A(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())
  I(state~M)<->I(state~P) U()*Lambda()*P(),U()*Lambda()*(1-P())

  # Quarantine (testing / contact tracing)
  E2(state~M)->E2(state~Q) kQ
  E2(state~P)->E2(state~Q) kQ
  E3(state~M)->E3(state~Q) kQ
  E3(state~P)->E3(state~Q) kQ
  E4(state~M)->E4(state~Q) kQ
  E4(state~P)->E4(state~Q) kQ
  E5(state~M)->E5(state~Q) kQ
  E5(state~P)->E5(state~Q) kQ
  A(state~M)->A(state~Q) kQ
  A(state~P)->A(state~Q) kQ
  I(state~M)->I(state~Q) kQfunc()
  I(state~P)->I(state~Q) kQ

  # Self-isolation (symptom awareness)
  I(state~M)->I(state~Q) jQfunc()
  I(state~P)->I(state~Q) jQ

  # Incubation (progression through the 5 Erlang stages)
  E1(state~M)->E2(state~M) kL
  E1(state~P)->E2(state~P) kL
  E2(state~M)->E3(state~M) kL
  E2(state~P)->E3(state~P) kL
  E2(state~Q)->E3(state~Q) kL
  E3(state~M)->E4(state~M) kL
  E3(state~P)->E4(state~P) kL
  E3(state~Q)->E4(state~Q) kL
  E4(state~M)->E5(state~M) kL
  E4(state~P)->E5(state~P) kL
  E4(state~Q)->E5(state~Q) kL
  E5(state~M)->A(state~M) kL*fA
  E5(state~P)->A(state~P) kL*fA
  E5(state~Q)->A(state~Q) kL*fA
  E5(state~M)->I(state~M) kL*(1-fA)
  E5(state~P)->I(state~P) kL*(1-fA)
  E5(state~Q)->I(state~Q) kL*(1-fA)

  # Immune clearance / recovery from mild disease
  A(state~M)->R() cA
  A(state~P)->R() cA
  A(state~Q)->R() cA
  I(state~M)->R() cIfunc()*(1-fH)
  I(state~P)->R() cI*(1-fH)
  I(state~Q)->R() cI*(1-fH)

  # Progression to severe disease / hospitalization
  I(state~M)->H() cIfunc()*fH
  I(state~P)->H() cI*fH
  I(state~Q)->H() cI*fH

  # Recovery from severe disease
  H()->R() cH*fR

end reaction rules

end model

begin actions

  generate_network({overwrite=>1})  # 26 species, 61 reactions

  #@note: |
  #  ODE-only. Time gating uses the counter() molecule as a deterministic clock
  #  (0->counter() at rate 1, so the observable t = 1 + simulation time); this and
  #  the if(t>=...) rate-law functions require deterministic integration -- under
  #  SSA the clock would be a Poisson process and NFsim does not support if() rate
  #  laws. The ~19.2M population is also far too large for stochastic simulation.

  # NYC daily new-case forecast

  #@protocol: |
  #  Integrate the compartmental ODE over the first COVID-19 wave (model day
  #  t = 0..170; day 0 = 2020-01-21) at the authors' published MAP. The fit
  #  observable fDCs_Cum is cumulative detected cases; its day-to-day increment
  #  (differenced in the notebook) is the daily new-case forecast, overlaid on the
  #  New York Times data -- reproducing the maximum-a-posteriori (median)
  #  trajectory of the NYC Bayesian forecast. Two social-distancing phases (onset
  #  sigma ~ day 33, tau1 ~ day 143) capture the broad spring-2020 plateau.

  #@figure: Fig. 3 in Lin et al. (2021)

  simulate({method=>"ode",suffix=>"ode",t_start=>0,t_end=>170,n_steps=>170,\
    print_functions=>1})

end actions
